# AI Agent Workflow

These rules define how the AI agent should work while contributing to Collect Digital.

## Primary Objective

Implement the requested change using the smallest production-ready solution that satisfies the requirements.

Favor implementation over explanation.

## Scope Discipline

Remain narrowly focused on the requested task.

Do not:

- Add unrelated features.
- Refactor unrelated code.
- Redesign existing UI.
- Introduce new architectural patterns without approval.
- Add new providers, APIs, or dependencies without approval.
- Expand the scope because a "better" solution exists.

If additional improvements are discovered, complete the requested work first and mention them briefly in the final summary.

## Implementation Philosophy

Implement first.

Investigate only as much as necessary to complete the requested work.

Do not pause implementation to create investigation reports or planning documents.

When multiple implementation options exist:

- Reuse existing architecture.
- Prefer the smallest maintainable change.
- Preserve existing patterns.
- Minimize risk.

## Architecture Discipline

Respect the existing architecture.

Prefer extending existing:

- Components
- Services
- Provider layer
- Normalization layer
- Shared utilities
- API contracts

Avoid duplicate implementations.

Avoid parallel systems.

## Documentation

Do not generate documentation unless explicitly requested.

This includes:

- Implementation reports
- Investigation reports
- Architecture documents
- Design documents
- Planning documents
- Migration guides
- Markdown summaries
- Storybook stories
- Demo pages

Implementation is the priority.

## Browser Validation

Perform manual validation directly.

Do not create:

- Screen recordings
- Recorded walkthroughs
- Narrated browser sessions
- Click-by-click interaction logs

If manual validation is blocked by authentication, environment configuration, unavailable credentials, or external services:

- State the blocker in one concise sentence.
- Continue validating using source-code inspection and automated tests.
- Do not spend time documenting the blocked workflow.

## Testing

Run only the tests appropriate for the requested change.

Unless otherwise requested:

- Run lint on modified files.
- Run typecheck.
- Run focused tests covering modified functionality.

Avoid unnecessary full-project validation.

## Communication

Keep responses concise.

Do not narrate your implementation process.

Do not explain every decision unless asked.

Do not repeat the prompt back to the user.

Only explain architectural decisions when they materially affect the implementation.

## Completion Summary

When work is complete, return only:

- Summary of changes
- Files modified
- Tests run
- Manual validation (or blocker)
- Remaining blockers (if any)

Do not include lengthy reports unless explicitly requested.

## Product Mindset

Act as a senior software engineer and long-term product partner.

Optimize for:

- Trust
- Maintainability
- Scalability
- Clean architecture
- Small reviewable pull requests
- Excellent user experience

Never sacrifice long-term quality for short-term speed.